66	12	2	2 \n \n \n \n \n \nContents \nA. \n4 \nB. \n5 \n1. Review of Literature ............................................................................................................... 5 \n1.1 The Challenge of Organizational Knowledge Loss .......................................................... 5 \n1.2 Decision Support Systems in Organizations ..................................................................... 6 \n1.3 Limitations of Traditional Decision Support Systems ...................................................... 7 \n1.4 Knowledge Graphs as Structured Knowledge Representation ......................................... 7 \n1.5 Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Grounded Responses ............................................. 7 \n1.6 Combining Knowledge Graphs with RAG ....................................................................... 8 \n1.7 Educational & Domain-Specific KG-RAG Systems ........................................................ 8 \n1.8 Literature Review Process ................................................................................................ 8 \n2. Review of Existing Systems ................................................................................................... 9 \n3. Research Gap .......................................................................................................................... 9 \nC. \n10 \nProblem Statement .................................................................................................................... 10 \nProject Objective:...................................................................................................................... 11 \nD. \n13 \nE. \n13 \n1. 14 \n2. 14 \nPlatform and Users ................................................................................................................ 13 \nTechnology Stack.................................................................................................................. 13 \nClient-Side Functionality ...................................................................................................... 13 \nServer-Side Functionality ..................................................................................................... 13	2026-07-31 03:30:03.141467
67	12	3	3 \n \nProject Accomplishments ..................................................................................................... 14 \nBoundaries and Limitations .................................................................................................. 14 \n3. 16 \n4. 17 \nGantt Chart: ........................................................................................................................... 17 \nF. \n21 \n1. 21 \n2. 21 \n3. 22 \nG. \n23 \n1. 23 \nR1 – Knowledge Graph Data Quality and Accuracy (Data Risk) ........................................ 21 \nR2 – Knowledge Graph and RAG Integration Complexity (Technical Risk) ...................... 21 \nR3 – Model Hallucination and Unsupported Generated Responses (Trust Risk) ................ 21 \nR4 – Query Performance and Scalability Limitations (Performance Risk) .......................... 22 \nR5 – Inadequate Technical Expertise or Tool Limitations (Operational Risk) .................... 22 \nR6 – Data Retrieval Inconsistency and Context Loss (Quality Risk) ................................... 22 \nR7 – System Integration Failure Between Frontend, Backend, and AI Services (System \nRisk) ...................................................................................................................................... 22 \nR8 – Timeline Overrun and Project Scope Expansion (Project Delivery Risk) ................... 22 \n2. 24 \nRisk Level Classification ...................................................................................................... 23 \nRisk Evaluation Table ........................................................................................................... 23 \nMitigation Strategies ............................................................................................................. 23 \nH. \n27 \nReferences ..................................................................................................................................... 29	2026-07-31 03:30:13.177364
68	12	4	4 \n \nA. General Information \n \nProject Title:    Development of a Web-Based Decision Support System for a Simulated \nOrganization Using Knowledge Graphs and Retrieval-Augmented Generation \n \nPoints of Contact \nMention assigned group name, List the principal individuals who may be contacted for information regarding the \nproject. \n \nGroup Name: Group 42 \n  \n \n \n \n---------------------------------------------            \n \nPosition \nFull Name \nPhone Number \nE-mail \nSupervisor \nSubit Timalsina   \nsubit@iimscollege.edu.np \n \n \n \n \nProject Members \nSamrat Jwala \nUprety \n9803359709 \nsamrat2080-\n0046@iimscollege.edu.np \nRayan Tolange \n9766385246 \nrayan2080-\n0115@iimscollege.edu.np \nRuby Bhuju \n9863446697 \n \nruby2080-\n0141@iimscollege.edu.np \nBibisha Shreesh 9746428773 \nbibisha2080-\n0162@iimscollege.edu.np \nSanjina \nShrestha \n9748292894 \nsanjina2080-\n0095@iimscollege.edu.np \nSupervisor Signature	2026-07-31 03:30:21.999883
69	12	5	5 \n \nB. Executive Summary \nEducational institutions increasingly rely on complex strategic and operational decisions made \nby academic leaders and administrators. Over time, the rationale, context, and outcomes of these \ndecisions are often lost due to staff turnover, fragmented documentation, and dependence on tacit \nknowledge. This erosion of institutional memory results in inconsistent decision making, \nrepeated errors, and limited organizational learning. Existing decision support systems in higher \neducation largely depend on static reports and structured data, which restricts their ability to \ncapture contextual relationships and support evidence-based reasoning over time. \n \nThis capstone project proposes the development of a web-based Decision Support System for a \nsimulated college environment that addresses these limitations through the integration of \nKnowledge Graphs and Retrieval-Augmented Generation. Organizational entities such as \ndepartments, strategies, actions, constraints, and outcomes are represented within a Knowledge \nGraph to preserve relationships and historical context. Structured decision data is stored in a \nrelational SQL database, while unstructured textual content is encoded as vector embeddings to \nenable semantic retrieval. A chat interface allows users to interact with the system using natural \nlanguage, with queries dynamically retrieving relevant information from multiple data sources to \nproduce grounded and context-aware responses. \n \nThe resulting prototype will demonstrate how AI-driven decision support can strengthen \ninstitutional memory, support learning from prior decisions, and reduce risks associated with \nknowledge loss in academic organizations. \n1. Literature Review \n1.1 The Challenge of Organizational Knowledge Loss \nHaving senior leaders exit organizations means that they leave behind them the knowledge that \nthey gained in decision making. This leaves loopholes that influence future decisions and \nsurvival of an organization. This tacit knowledge can hardly be gathered with the help of \ntraditional knowledge management systems (Taherdoost, 2023) (Neiroukh, 2024). \nThe theory of organizational learning also points out that learning that is stored in routines, \nprocesses and personal knowledge forms the basis of an organization in adapting (Miron-\nSpektor, 2011). The loss of experienced personnel means that organizations lose not only formal \nknowledge recorded in documents but also contextual information about how and why things \nwere done the way that they were and what forced one to make a move. This loss of knowledge \nwill have a direct effect on the capability of an organization to learn through previous experience \nand implement the same in addressing future challenges. \n1.2 Decision Support Systems in Organizations	2026-07-31 03:30:44.057292
70	12	6	6 \n \nThe computer relied information systems which can also be called as decision support \nsystem(DDS),it uses the information of an organization and compile analyze those information \nhelping in making the decision.It is an amalgamation of data, analyze the whole model as well as \ninteractive interfaces help in making the decision by solving structured and unstructured \nproblems.Use of DSS has help in planning and making the strategy for an organization, \nperformance appraisal and even learning institutions. \n \n \n1.2.1 Decision Support Systems in Educational Institutions \n \nDDS has increasingly gained a lot of popularity amongs the academic institutions for enabling \nand helping administrators and other academic schedulers  in making decisions on various \nstrategies and effective teaching cycle.The authors of this case study discovered that DDS can \nalso be applied in the context of an educational field whereby the operational and scholarly data \nis used to make decision about instruction.It showcases that DDS has the capacity of improving \nthe efficiency and accuracy of decision at the institution because of the fact that it clears the need \nto make judgements based on a subject(Society, 2022) \n \n1.2.2 Big Data–Driven Decision Support Systems \nOne of the studies published in the Journal of Electrical Systems analyzed the use of the concept \nof big data analytics, in the decision support system context, as academic data and information \ncontinue to swell exponentially.One of the studies proposed the use of a big data-based DSS for \neducation management that would calculate the vast amounts of academic performance data to \nenable academic plan and prognosis. The findings indicated that DSS using big data and early \nintervention strategies can be employed to predict academic success. (Systems, 2024) \n1.2.3 Importance of DSS in Higher Education \nUsing Decision Support Systems (DSS) within a management capacity represents an avenue for \nHigher Education institutions to use information technologies to aid their leaders in integrating \nthe application of information technology into their decision making processes. Research \nconducted previously indicates that the use of DSS by Administrators will facilitate a more \nefficient allocation of resources, streamline the Strategic Planning process and provide assistance \nin meeting student needs through enhanced access to relevant business analytics and actionable \nfeedback. Interestingly, when Higher Education institutions utilize DSS in their academic and \nadministrative functions, they have more efficient operations with less need for manual \nprocessing, as well as being able to base their decisions on factual evidence. (Zhang & Goyal, \n2024) \n1.2.4 Decision Models and Methods in DSS	2026-07-31 03:30:55.05832
72	12	7	7 \n \nThere have been many studies trying to improve the Decision Support Systems (DSS), through \nthe addition of structured decision-making models. In one study published in 2023, researchers \nused the Simple Additive Weighting (SAW) technique in the educational version of a DSS that \ncan help solve multiple criteria decision-making (MCDM). This DSS was evaluated by \ncomparing MCDM decisions made using methodologies with DSS against MCDM with no DSS. \nThe study concluded that DSS that include structured decision-making methodologies provide a \nclearer and more accurate outcome than an MCDM without DSS.(Hak, et al., 2023) \n1.2.5 Predictive Decision Support Systems Using Educational Data Mining \nThere have been many studies trying to improve the Decision Support Systems (DSS), through \nthe addition of structured decision-making models. In one study published in 2023, researchers \nused the Simple Additive Weighting (SAW) technique in the educational version of a DSS that \ncan help solve multiple criteria decision-making (MCDM). This DSS was evaluated by \ncomparing MCDM decisions made using methodologies with DSS against MCDM with no DSS. \nThe study concluded that DSS that include structured decision-making methodologies provide a \nclearer and more accurate outcome than an MCDM without DSS.(Saa, 2016) \n \n1.2.6 Data Warehouse and Business Intelligence–Based DSS \nOne other option for enabling analytics scalability and practical use is data warehousing -based \nDSS architecture. Created in 2021, it relies on data from independent educational marts. It's a \ndata-centric DSS that leverages business intelligence reporting and trend-analysis tools. This \npaper has shown that decision support systems (DSS) that are backed by well-designed data \nwarehouses can be trusted to be useful in schools and other learning environments.( (Hamoud, et \nal., 2021) \n1.3 Limitations of Traditional Decision Support Systems \n \nThe limitations associated with legacy decision support systems are that they lack agility to \nadjust to dynamic decision environments since they focus heavily on rigid processes like formal \nreporting methods and rules-based algorithms. They fail to capture and replicate tacit knowledge \nharvested from historical organizational decisions. As such, traditional DSS can not comprehend \nnatural language and therefore cannot be utilized by non-technical users. Above all, this \nlimitation points to the gap that necessitates continued efforts in intelligent knowledge-driven \nsolutions that aid context-based reasoning and decision-making. \n1.4 Knowledge Graphs as Structured Knowledge Representation \n \nKnowledge graphs use semantic technologies and graph theories to represent entities, relations, \nor attributes among interconnected systems  (Gutiérrez, 2021).They rather than rely on flat \ndatabases, chart how one concept relates to another to allow for reasoning and inference. This	2026-07-31 03:31:09.087679
80	12	11	11 \n \nColleges and other educational institutions often experience challenges in the reuse and \npreservation of strategic and operational decision-making knowledge. Information about past \ndecisions made by the academic and administrative staff is mostly fragmented across different \nsources, including reports, policies, emails, and the knowledge held by the staff. This has made it \ndifficult for these institutions to retain knowledge about past decisions, which has resulted in \ninconsistencies in the decision-making process. This has often come at a price, especially when \nthe key staff members depart the institution. This is often referred to as corporate amnesia. \n(Wikipedia, 2024)   \n \nEvidence 1: 42% of Institutional Knowledge Is Unique to Each Individual \nFact: When an employee quits their peers cannot perform 42 percent of their job due to the fact \nthat such a person has a unique knowledge (Panopto, 2018) that he or she does not share with \nanybody. \nSource: surveying over 1,000 workers in the U.S. the first study of its kind to examine workplace \nknowledge and productivity reveals that half of the knowledge in institutions is individualized \nand that 12 to 15 years on average, one large enterprise is wasting $47 million in productivity \ndue to poor knowledge sharing (Panopto (n.d.), Knowledge and Productivity Workplace Report). \nThis has often come at a price, especially when the key staff members depart the institution. \n(Galdamez, 2025) \n \nEvidence 2: Peer-Reviewed: 91-Study Systematic Review of Knowledge Loss from Turnover \nFact: Past academic literature ascertains that the turnover of organizational staff members leads \nto quantifiable loss of routines, memory and absorptive ability. \nSource: A two-part synthesis of systematic literature published in The Learning Organization \n(Emerald Publishing) based on 91 empirical studies on knowledge loss induced by turnover \nfound that knowledge loss through turnover results in 