74	12	8	8 \n \nmethod has proved effective in sectors such as healthcare and manufacturing where how one \nentity relates to another is critical in decision-making.  (Chandak, 2022) (Bader, 2020) \n \n1.5 Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Grounded Responses \nLarge language models often generate inaccurate information when they lack domain-specific \nknowledge. RAG addresses this by retrieving relevant information from external sources before \ngenerating responses (Lewis, 2020).This method bases the result on real data as compared to \nsimply relying on the training of the model. Nevertheless, conventional RAG recovers separate \ntext fragments, depending on their similarity, whereas the connection between chunks of \ninformation is absent. . (Gao, 2023). \n \n1.6 Combining Knowledge Graphs with RAG \n \nLinking Knowledge Graphs to RAG can create business opportunities. Previous studies show \nthat using knowledge graphs with RAG improves how well information is found and how \naccurate responses are. For example, the IKEDS framework achieved an accuracy rate of 85.7 \npercent in financial, health, and supply chain decisions by combining structured knowledge with \ngenerative capabilities (Wang, 2025). ). Similarly, KG2RAG discovered that using knowledge-\ngraph-based expansion and reorganization led to better retrieval F1 scores, increasing from 0.357 \nto 0.436 compared to retrieval using semantics (Zhu, 2025).Combining both methods shows \nconnections between retrieved content at the fact level, which makes answers more logical and \nrelevant to the context. These methods are often used for information access in general, and they \ncan also assist with retrieval and providing necessary responses. A lot of time is spent keeping up \nwith and rediscovering organizational decision-making information, where past knowledge, \nconnections, and outcomes are crucial for future decisions. \n \n1.7 Educational & Domain-Specific KG-RAG Systems \nThe application of KG-RAG in domain-specific systems and instructional systems has also been \nexamined in other recent works. When responding to academic inquiries, the application of KG-\nRAG and retrieval-augmented generation as well as ordered didactic information known as KA-\nRAG has shown even stronger reasoning power.The startling accuracy of 91.4 has been \ndemonstrated by KA-RAG. The proficiency of dialogue-based learning systems with KG-RAG \nhas been proven.A systematic linkage of various educational facts has been made possible by \nthe cross-domain expertise in graph design.  \n \n \n1.8 Literature Review Process	2026-07-31 03:31:24.937908
77	12	9	9 \n \nA systematic and structured search was performed to locate the body of scholarly work on the \ntopics of decision support systems, knowledge graph, retrieval-augmented generation, and \norganizational knowledge management. Academic databases such as Google Scholar, IEEE \nXplore, ACM Digital Library, Science Direct, and Springer Link were used to find peer-\nreviewed journal articles, conference proceedings, and technical reports. Retrieval-augmented \ngeneration (RAG), educational data mining, decision support systems, loss of organizational \nknowledge, knowledge graphs, and AI in education management were the search terms. \n \nThe studies have been chosen according to their relevance to the field of decision-making \nsupport, AI-based knowledge representation, or the use of knowledge graphs or RAG \ntechnologies in an organizational or teaching setting. The focus was on the recent materials \n(2016-2025) to provide up-to-date technological knowledge, and the earlier foundational studies \nwere also provided where needed to provide theoretical background. Articles with unclear \nmethodologies, lack of credibility or directness concerning knowledge retention or decision \nsupport were sifted out. Overall, out of the over thirty screened papers, fourteen main studies \nwere chosen to be analyzed and synthesized in this review. \n2. Review of Existing Systems \nThere are some systems that try to mix knowledge graphs with RAG. The GraphRag used in \nMicrosoft utilizes community-based graph summary but makes use of pre-summaries that restrict \nflexibility. LightRAG is an alternative with light weight, yet is less accurate (F1: 0.293). IKEDS \nframework offers the best opportunity of total integration that involves five interconnected parts \nand has been used with good outcomes in a variety of domains. KG2RAG pays particular \nattention to retrieval enhancement by means of the expansion of the chunk with the use of a \ngraph. A comparison is made of these systems in Table 2.   Existing decision support systems \nmainly rely on static reports and predefined rule-based logic. Such systems work well for \nstructured data but perform poorly when dealing with tacit knowledge and complex \norganizational relationships. Additionally, traditional DSS do not support natural language \nquerying, making them less user-friendly for non-technical users. These limitations highlight the \nneed for intelligent, AI-driven decision support approaches. \nTable 2: Comparison of existing KG-RAG systems. \n \nSystem \nStrengths \nLimitations \nGraphRAG \nGlobal context awareness, query-\nfocused summarization \nHigh computational cost, pre-\ncomputed summaries limit \nadaptability	2026-07-31 03:32:04.568117
78	12	10	10 \n \nLightRAG \nFast processing, lower resource \nrequirements \nLower accuracy (F1: 0.293), limited \nreasoning depth \nIKEDS \n85.7% accuracy, cross-domain \nreasoning, explainability \nComplex architecture, high \nknowledge engineering effort \nKG2RAG \nImproved retrieval (F1: 0.436), \ncoherent context \nFocused on QA tasks, limited \ndecision support features \n \n3. Research Gap \nIn addition, most of the existing studies evaluate their methods on a benchmark/real company \nbasis, and there is minimal research on simulated organizational settings to validate their systems \nand to experiment. Although current systems have encouraging scores in the field of question-\nanswering exercises, they have not explicitly focused on the knowledge retention to decision \nmaking in an organization. Recent methods are missing: (Taherdoost, 2023) purpose-built \nontologies to represent structures of organizations such as departments, strategies, and other \noutcomes; (Neiroukh, 2024) user-friendly web interfaces on querying past decisions; and \n(Gutiérrez, 2021) the easily accessible web interfaces. This project will seal these gaps by \ncoming up with a web-based decision support system that will suit the organization's knowledge \nmanagement. \nBased on the literature reviewed, traditional decision support systems fall short in capturing tacit \norganizational knowledge, while new techniques such as knowledge graphs and retrieval-\naugmented generation bear a high potential for improving contextual reasoning and information \nretrieval. However, the current applications are mostly intended for question-answer or general \ninformation retrieval purposes, not for maintaining the history of organizational decisions to be \nused later for strategic decisions. \nThe proposed project is intended to bridge this knowledge gap and create a web-based intelligent \ndecision support system that integrates knowledge graph representation and retrieval-augmented \ngeneration methods. It will store the organization's past decision-making, situational connections, \nand outcomes in a structured graph format and grant natural language querying through an \ninterface utilizing artificial intelligence. By integrating structured organizational knowledge with \nthe generative functions of artificial intelligence, this project hopes to enhance organizational \nknowledge retention, improve evidence-based decision making, and enable friendly analytical \ntools for the primarily academic or organizational management institutional clients. \nC. Project Purpose \nProblem Statement	2026-07-31 03:32:21.766816
82	12	12	12 \n \nknowledge, institutions can be vulnerable to repeated errors, lost chances and opportunities and \noverall reduced effectiveness. \n \nProject Objective: \nThe project seeks to develop a centralized decision support system that can be accessed via the \ninternet for a simulated college, addressing the aforementioned challenges. In particular, the \nobjectives are: \n1. Design a web-based decision support system that can capture and retain decision-making \nknowledge for various departments. \n2. Implement a SQL database for the decision-making knowledge for strategic and \noperational decisions. \n3. Implement a Knowledge Graph for modeling organizational entities, such as strategies, \nactions, constraints, and outcomes. \n4. Implement the Retrieval-Augmented Generation model with the Knowledge Graph for \nprecise, contextual, and transparent decision-making. \n5. Implement the capability for users to query historical decision-making via natural \nlanguage, retrieving relevant historical cases. \n6. Analyze historical decision outcomes for successful strategies and unsuccessful \nstrategies. \n7. Demonstrate the potential for decision support systems based on artificial intelligence for \norganizational learning, continuity, and risk minimization. \n  \nNo. \nProposed Functionality \nProblems Solved/ Opportunities \n1 \nCentralized Knowledge \nrepository \n \n \n \nSolves the issue of scattered information on decisions made, \nthus avoiding the loss of institutional memory. \n2 \nKnowledge Graph-Based \nModeling	2026-07-31 03:32:41.681602
