101	12	23	23 \n \nBibisha Shreesh \nFrontend and Graph \nVisualization Engineer \nPrepares visual maps and charts indicating the \nrelationships between various decisions.  \nFacilitates the construction of the web site so \nthat information that is complicated can be \npresented openly.  \nAids in the process of searching and retrieving \ninformation within the system to present itself \nto the user. \nSanjina Shrestha \nData Integration, KGE and \nSystem Validation Lead \nGathers, distributes, and organizes the \ninformation upon which the system is \ninformed.  \nMaintains the knowledge maps to keep the \nfacts within the system correct.  \nMakes end checks to detect mistakes and \nensures that the system is functioning \nproperly. \n \nG. Risk Analysis \nIn this section, the major risks to the development, integration, and deployment of the proposed \nWeb-Based Decision Support System (DSS) in consideration of the Knowledge Graphs and \nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) are outlined. In consideration of the fact that the DSS \nwill be dependent on knowledge representation, artificial intelligence retrieval, and the generation \nof large language models, there are likely to be a number of risks. \n \nThese risks are measured in terms of probability and impact, and suggestions are made to mitigate \nthe risks of incorrect decision recommendations, integration, and delays in project delivery. \n1. Potential Risks \nR1 – Knowledge Graph Data Quality and Accuracy (Data Risk) \nThe achievement of the DSS depends entirely on the accuracy and comprehensiveness of \nthe data used to create the knowledge graph. \nR2 – Knowledge Graph and RAG Integration Complexity (Technical Risk)	2026-07-31 03:34:31.880096
103	12	24	24 \n \nThe integration of query systems with other systems may present risks of inefficient \nquery systems and inconsistent data retrieval. \nR3 – Model Hallucination and Unsupported Generated Responses (Trust Risk) \nThe responses generated by the Large Language Model used by the RAG may not be \nsupported by the data retrieved from the knowledge graph. \nR4 – Query Performance and Scalability Limitations (Performance Risk) \nThe performance of the knowledge graph may slow down with an increase in the size of \nthe graph. \nR5 – Inadequate Technical Expertise or Tool Limitations (Operational Risk) \nThe technical team may face challenges due to inadequate knowledge of knowledge \ngraph technology and the RAG framework used by the AI deployment environment. \nR6 – Data Retrieval Inconsistency and Context Loss (Quality Risk) \nThere is a risk of data retrieval being inconsistent, and the DSS may not be able to fetch \nthe relevant context, resulting in a response that may be incomplete or irrelevant to the \ndata entered in the DSS. \nR7 – System Integration Failure Between Frontend, Backend, and AI Services (System \nRisk) \nThere is a risk of failure in the integration of the user interface, backend APIs, graph \ndatabases, and AI services, resulting in a failure of the system itself. \nR8 – Timeline Overrun and Project Scope Expansion (Project Delivery Risk) \nThere is a risk of project delays or scope expansion because of the integration of multiple \nsophisticated technologies in the project. \n2. Risk Assessment Methodology \nThe risks are assessed based on the following metrics: \nLikelihood (L) \n1 = Rare \n2 = Unlikely \n3 = Possible \n4 = Likely \n5 = Almost Certain \nImpact (I)	2026-07-31 03:34:42.094476
106	12	26	26 \n \nR4 Performanc\ne \nSlower Graph Queries and Scalability \nIssues \n3 3 9 \nMediu\nm \nR5 Operational \nLack of Expertise or Tool Limitations \n2 1 2 \nLow \nR6 Quality \nInconsistency in Retrieval and Loss of \nContext \n2 2 4 \nLow \nR7 System \nIntegration Failure Between Frontend, \nBackend, and AI \n2 2 4 \nLow \nR8 Delivery \nExcessive Timeline and Scope Growth \n1 2 2 \nLow \n \nThe following sections outline the mitigation strategies for the project, grouped under \nPreventive, Detective, and Corrective measures to ensure a robust and reliable system: \nTechnical & Data Integrity (R1, R2, & R3) \nTo ensure data integrity in the Knowledge Graph (KG), preventive measures are in place to use \ncurated data and perform data normalization. For detectively addressing the data, the system will \nrequire human verification of the relationships between entities and perform periodic checks to \nmaintain consistency in the data. \nIn terms of the integration complexity of KG-RAG, the strategy proposes a modular architecture \nand standard APIs to avoid any bottlenecks in integration. Continuous testing of the graph \nqueries and retrieval modules is carried out to monitor integration, and corrective actions of \nrefactoring and fallback are undertaken to ensure the system is operational in the event of \nintegration failures. In terms of the mitigation of Model Hallucination, the strategy proposes \nlimiting the generated output to the retrieved knowledge, and the use of advanced prompt \nengineering techniques is undertaken to improve accuracy. Accuracy is detected by cross-\nchecking the output with the source, and corrections are undertaken through the use of \nconfidence indicators and mandatory citations. \nPerformance, Scalability, & Retrieval (R4, R6, & R7) \nIn terms of Performance and Scalability, the scope of the system is initially limited to simulated \ndata, and optimization of indexing and queries is undertaken to ensure Performance and \nScalability. Performance benchmarking and load testing are undertaken as the primary detective \ntools, and caching and database query optimization are undertaken as corrective actions in the \nevent of any latencies in Performance and Scalability.	2026-07-31 03:35:02.370006
