109	12	27	27 \n \nRetrieval Inconsistency is averted by improving the quality of embeddings and utilizing a hybrid \nsearch strategy. Retrieval Inconsistency is evaluated by executing benchmark queries, which \nenables making corrective changes to ranking strategies and data preprocessing techniques. To \nprevent System Integration Failures, API communication standards and services are defined. \nContinuous system testing helps detect silos and failures, which are fixed by strengthening error \nlogging and fallback services. \nResource & Project Management (R5 & R8) \nTo address Limited Technical Expertise, open-source tools are utilized with good documentation \nand adequate time provided for experimentation by the team. Periodic technical reviews and \nprototyping help detect technical knowledge gaps. As a corrective measure for Limited \nTechnical Expertise when technical issues are insurmountable, open-source resources are utilized \nand the tool stack is simplified. \nTo prevent Timeline Overruns, the project is implemented by first creating an MVP with defined \nproject milestones. Progress is monitored weekly by performing weekly reviews to detect delays. \nAs a corrective measure for Timeline Overruns, the project reallocation of resources for \ndevelopment is performed to prevent delays in the delivery of the main system. \nH. Appendices \nReviewed Literature \nConcept/Idea \nHow are we different / How can we \nadd value \nTaherdoost & \nMadanchian (2023) – AI \nand Knowledge \nManagement: A \nPartnership \nResearch the application of \nArtificial Intelligence in \nknowledge management \nsystems. Demonstrates ways \nin which AI can make \nknowledge retrieval, sharing \nand decision-making more \nefficient and points to \norganizational issues in \nmanaging large volumes of \nknowledge. \nSpecifically, our project will involve \nthe incorporation of RAG and \nKnowledge Graphs into a Decision \nSupport System to apply AI-based \nknowledge management. As \nopposed to general knowledge \nmanagement discussions, our system \nemploys structured organizational \nknowledge and semantic relationship \nto offer real time explainable \ndecision support. \nNeiroukh et al. (2024) – \nAI Capability and \nOrganizational \nPerformance \nResearches the role of AI \ncapabilities in enhancing \ndecision-making, operational \nperformance and knowledge \nOur system takes these theoretical \nAI abilities and transforms them into \na practical web-based DSS \nprototype. It integrates generative AI \nmodels with knowledge graphs to	2026-07-31 03:35:11.652841
111	12	28	28 \n \ndiscovery to enhance \norganizational performance. \ncome up with valuable \nrecommendations that are aligned \nwith specific instances of \norganizational decisions. \nGutiérrez & Sequeda \n(2021) – Knowledge \nGraphs \nDefines knowledge graphs as \nsemantic graph-based models \nthat enhance the connectivity \nof the information and the \ncontextual representation of \nknowledge by modeling \nentities and relationships. \nOur project adopts the knowledge \ngraphs as the primary data modeling \nlayer in order to represent \norganizational procedures, policies, \nand situations of decisions. This can \nbe used to retrieve relationships and \nincreases explainability of decisions. \nChandak et al. (2022) – \nBuilding a Knowledge \nGraph for Precision \nMedicine \nShows how knowledge graphs \ncould be employed to aid \nclinical decision-making in \nhealthcare through the \ncombination of heterogeneous \nmedical data.  \nThough the focus of this paper was \non healthcare applications, our \nproject shows that the Knowledge \nGraph methodology is not only \nlimited to domain-specific medical \napplications but can also be applied \nto organizational decision \nenvironments. \nBader et al. (2020) – A \nKnowledge Graph for \nIndustry 4.0 \nDescribes how knowledge \ngraphs are being applied in \nmanufacturing to assist \nintelligent industrial decision \nsystems and combine \nproduction data. \nOur system extends the application \nof Knowledge Graphs outside of \nknowledge intensive organizational \ndecision support to industrial \nautomation. To be able to synthesize \ninformation and generate \nrecommendations, we integrate KG \nwith RAG-based generative AI. \nLewis et al. (2020) – \nRetrieval-Augmented \nGeneration for \nKnowledge-Intensive \nNLP Tasks \nIntroduces the RAG \narchitecture that integrates \nboth text generation and \ninformation retrieval to \nimprove contextual response \nas well as factual accuracy of \nNLP systems. \nKnowledge Graph-guided context \nenrichment and integration of \nstructured organizational data are \nused in our project to enhance the \nquality of retrieval and to keep RAG \nas the main reasoning mechanism. \nGao et al. (2023) – \nRetrieval-Augmented \nGives a detailed overview of \nRAG architectures and \noutlines the shortcomings, \nincluding the ability to retrieve \nOur system tries to resolve those \nlimitations by applying Knowledge \nGraph-based retrieval that enhances \nthe contextual understanding,	2026-07-31 03:35:17.819986
113	12	29	29 \n \nGeneration for LLMs: A \nSurvey \nsingle document fragments \nwithout recording the semantic \nconnections between them. \nrelationship awareness, and multi-\nhop reasoning. \nWang et al. (2025) – \nConstruction of \nIntelligent Decision \nSupport Systems \n(IKEDS) \nIntroduces a smart DSS \narchitecture that combines AI \ntechniques with knowledge \nintegration and proves to be \nvery accurate across a variety \nof fields. \nThis framework is extended into our \nproject where modern LLM-based \nRAG architecture and Knowledge \nGraphs are incorporated to increase \nthe explainability, scalability, and \ndomain adaptability in a simulated \norganizational environment. \nZhu et al. (2025) – \nKnowledge Graph-\nGuided Retrieval \nAugmented Generation \n(KG2RAG) \nProposes KG-directed chunk \ngrowth procedures to enhance \nthe accuracy of retrieval and \nthe depth of reasoning, with \nbetter F1 scores in benchmark \nQA datasets. \nOur system uses KG-RAG \nintegration in a similar way but in \ndecision support cases rather than \nQA tasks and does not simply \nanswer questions but produces \nactionable recommendations. \nLi et al. (2025) – KA-\nRAG: Integrating \nKnowledge Graphs and \nAgentic Retrieval-\nAugmented Generation \nfor Intelligent \nEducational QA \nIntroduces the idea of an \neducational QA system \ncombining Knowledge Graphs \nwith agentic retrieval system, \nwhich can enhance a higher \nretrieval accuracy and domain-\nspecific question answering. \nKG-RAG methodology is applied to \norganizational DSS (and not \neducational QA) in our project. We \nconcentrate on strategic decision \nsupport, domain modeled knowledge \nand recommendation generation that \ncan be interacted with by the user. \nZhang et al. (2025) – \nGenerating Vicarious \nDialogue for Online \nLearning Using KG-RAG \nShows application of KG-\nenhanced RAG to create \ninteractive learning dialogue \nand creation of contextual \ncontent. \nKG-RAG is used in our system not 