264	\N	12	\N	\N	\N	77	document_chunk	1	The studies have been chosen according to their relevance to the field of decision-making \nsupport, AI-based knowledge representation, or the use of knowledge graphs or RAG \ntechnologies in an organizational or teaching setting. The focus was on the recent materials \n(2016-2025) to provide up-to-date technological knowledge, and the earlier foundational studies \nwere also provided where needed to provide theoretical background. Articles with unclear \nmethodologies, lack of credibility or directness concerning knowledge retention or decision \nsupport were sifted out. Overall, out of the over thirty screened papers, fourteen main studies \nwere chosen to be analyzed and synthesized in this review. \n2. Review of Existing Systems \nThere are some systems that try to mix knowledge graphs with RAG. The GraphRag used in \nMicrosoft utilizes community-based graph summary but makes use of pre-summaries that restrict \nflexibility. LightRAG is an alternative with light weight, yet is less accurate (F1: 0.293). IKEDS \nframework offers the best opportunity of total integration that involves five interconnected parts \nand has been used with good outcomes in a variety of domains. KG2RAG pays particular \nattention to retrieval enhancement by means of the expansion of the chunk with the use of a \ngraph. A comparison is made of these systems in Table 2.   Existing decision support systems \nmainly rely on static reports and predefined rule-based logic. Such systems work well for \nstructured data but perform poorly when dealing with tacit knowledge and complex \norganizational relationships. Additionally, traditional DSS do not support natural language \nquerying, making them less user-friendly for non-technical users. These limitations highlight the \nneed for intelligent, AI-driven decision support approaches. \nTable 2: Comparison of existing KG-RAG systems.	[0.027809685,0.0682025,-0.18333732,-0.07106134,0.04022145,-0.007086594,0.